LESSON 18: CHANGING THE CHECKBOX COLOR AND ITEM TEXT WHEN CLICKED
STRIKETHROUGH FOR PURCHASED ITEMS
================================================================================
PART 1: OVERVIEW OF THE LESSON
================================================================================

WHAT YOU'LL LEARN IN THIS LESSON
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This lesson focuses on providing visual feedback when a grocery item is
marked as purchased.

You will understand:

• Styling UI based on React state

• Applying conditional CSS classes

• Creating a purchased visual state

• Adding strikethrough text

• Changing item-name color

• Changing card background

• Reducing visual emphasis for purchased items

• Styling a checked checkbox

• Using accent-color

• Adding hover and focus feedback

• Using multiple visual indicators

• Keeping visual state synchronized with React state

• Supporting reduced-motion preferences


WHY THIS LESSON IS IMPORTANT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The toggle logic from Lessons 16-17 already changes:

item.purchased


Now the UI should clearly communicate that state.

The complete flow becomes:

USER CLICKS CHECKBOX
        ↓
onToggleBought(item.id)
        ↓
App Updates State
        ↓
item.purchased Changes
        ↓
GroceryItem Re-renders
        ↓
Conditional Class Changes
        ↓
CSS Changes Appearance
        ↓
User Sees Purchased State


Without visual feedback:

State Changed
   ↓
User May Not Notice


With visual feedback:

State Changed
   ↓
Checkbox Checked
   +
Strikethrough
   +
Background Change
   +
Reduced Emphasis
   ↓
State Is Obvious


================================================================================
PART 2: UNDERSTANDING VISUAL STATE CHANGES
================================================================================

WHAT IS A VISUAL STATE?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A visual state is how the UI represents application state.

For the Grocery List:

STATE:

item.purchased


has two values:

false

or:

true


These can produce two visual presentations.


================================================================================
UNPURCHASED
================================================================================

item.purchased:

false


Visual state:

• Checkbox unchecked

• Item name normal

• Darker text

• White card

• Full emphasis


================================================================================
PURCHASED
================================================================================

item.purchased:

true


Visual state:

• Checkbox checked

• Item name crossed out

• Softer text

• Softer background

• Reduced emphasis


================================================================================
VISUAL STATE TABLE
================================================================================

+----------------------+-------------------------+--------------------------+
| PROPERTY             | UNPURCHASED             | PURCHASED                |
+----------------------+-------------------------+--------------------------+
| Checkbox             | Unchecked               | Checked                  |
| Checkbox Accent      | Browser/default state   | Green accent             |
| Name Decoration      | Normal                  | Line-through             |
| Name Color           | Dark                    | Softer gray              |
| Background           | White                   | Light gray/green-gray     |
| Border               | Neutral                 | Softer purchased border  |
| Emphasis             | Full                    | Reduced                   |
+----------------------+-------------------------+--------------------------+


================================================================================
IMPORTANT ACCESSIBILITY PRINCIPLE
================================================================================

Do NOT communicate purchased state using color alone.

For example:

BAD:

Purchased
 ↓
Green only


BETTER:

Purchased
 ↓
Checked Checkbox
+
Strikethrough
+
Background Change
+
Color Change


The checked state remains meaningful even if the user cannot distinguish
the color difference.


================================================================================
STATE VS STYLE
================================================================================

React owns:

item.purchased


React/JSX decides:

Which CSS class should be present?


CSS decides:

What should that class look like?


FLOW:

React State
    ↓
Conditional Class
    ↓
CSS Selector
    ↓
Visual Appearance


